Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||09-18-2000||03:56 PM||||A friend has a 4.3L that I can get for free... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">     Do I need the whole v-8 kit for the 4.3L  ????  Will it be worth it to do this conversion???  ALL OPINION'S NEEDED!!<P>                   sTEVE<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||mrfixit58||09-18-2000||04:19 PM||||This is one of those "tweeners"... except for the free motor, the cost to convert the Fiero to 4.3 is the same as the SBC V8. However, people talk about how much fun their car is to drive with 180hp, 200hp, 225 hp 250hp, etc. All these numbers I've seen quoted with 2.8, 3.1, 3.4, 3400, 3800. I've even seen a couple of these numbers quoted with people who have tired SBC V8s. <P>All those numbers can be achieved using the 4.3. Check with your local Chevrolet parts guy and ask him if you can see the cataloge that has all the performance goodies for the 4.3. You'll be very surprised. <P>The flip side is that you can make all the above performance numbers with the SBC and a LOT more... cheap. There are down sides such as cooling, work space, and especially weight.<P>If your a cornering freak, you'll want to lighten the V8 to help with the handling. Expect to spend beteen $500 (swap meet score) to $1500 on aluminum intake, water pump and heads and to move the battery up front.<P>If you want a little extra room and to save a few pounds, the 4.3 could be trick. I have heard tell of excess vibraton from older versions of the motor so beware.<P>But ya know what they say... there's no substitute for cubic inches!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
